{"level":20,"time":"2026-04-26T13:05:08.547Z","script":"llm","phase":"llm-request","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v3.2","temperature":0,"message_count":2,"messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You produce only valid JSON for a cleaned source note. Clean and normalize the source content without losing materially useful information. Do not invent facts, links, names, dates, numbers, or claims not present in the source. Remove only boilerplate, navigation, ads, duplicated text, formatting noise, and irrelevant wrapper text. Do not propose wiki mutations. This step only prepares the wiki/sources note content. Write all generated text (summary, raw_context, extracted_claims, open_questions) in Spanish, regardless of the source language. Do NOT translate proper nouns, names of people, companies, tools, books, films, or articles — keep them in their original form. Return a JSON object with summary, raw_context, extracted_claims, and open_questions."},{"role":"user","content":"{\n  \"source_payload\": {\n    \"source_id\": \"src-2026-04-26-web-1d57c08f4a\",\n    \"source_kind\": \"web\",\n    \"captured_at\": \"2026-04-26T13:05:03.193Z\",\n    \"raw_path\": \"raw/web/2026-04-26-youtube-new-diet-heals-gut-in-30-days-important-new-research-37d7eb32.md\",\n    \"title\": \"New Diet Heals Gut in 30 Days (Important New Research)\",\n    \"content\": \"# New Diet Heals Gut in 30 Days (Important New Research)\\n\\n## Source\\n- URL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ElUfYaHrzyU\\n- YouTube video ID: ElUfYaHrzyU\\n- Caption track: English (en, asr)\\n\\n## Transcript\\n[00:00:00] Could an enzyme deficiency explain why\\n[00:00:02] you have food intolerances causing\\n[00:00:04] bloating, loose bowels, reflux, joint\\n[00:00:06] pain, skin eruptions? Really, any number\\n[00:00:08] of symptoms that seem to be triggered by\\n[00:00:11] foods, namely an enzyme deficiency known\\n[00:00:13] as sucrace isomaltase.\\n[00:00:16] And a version of this enzyme deficiency\\n[00:00:20] may affect anywhere from 8 to 25% of the\\n[00:00:24] population. Thankfully, a diet exists\\n[00:00:27] for this enzyme deficiency and it's\\n[00:00:29] actually been studied against one of the\\n[00:00:32] best gut healing diets we have and shown\\n[00:00:34] to be as if not more effective. So\\n[00:00:38] perhaps we could say this might be the\\n[00:00:40] best diet for gut health. Let's break\\n[00:00:43] down what this enzyme deficiency is,\\n[00:00:46] what the corresponding diet is, known as\\n[00:00:48] the SSRD diet, and how this diet applies\\n[00:00:52] and can be helpful for those with, of\\n[00:00:54] course, this enzyme deficiency, but even\\n[00:00:56] go as far as supporting underlying\\n[00:00:59] fungal overgrowth, bacterial overgrowth,\\n[00:01:01] leaky gut, and health and balance in the\\n[00:01:04] microbiota. And if you're new here,\\n[00:01:06] welcome. I'm Michael Rusho. I'm a doctor\\n[00:01:09] in clinical practice. I also work as an\\n[00:01:11] adjunct professor and as a clinical\\n[00:01:13] researcher with a few papers published\\n[00:01:16] and more in the works. Okay, so let's\\n[00:01:18] talk more about this enzyme deficiency.\\n[00:01:20] The acronym is CSID or congenital\\n[00:01:24] sucrace isomaltase deficiency. Now,\\n[00:01:27] previously this was thought to be rare\\n[00:01:30] 1% perhaps less of the population. But\\n[00:01:32] as I alluded to a moment ago, more\\n[00:01:34] contemporary research is finding that\\n[00:01:36] this enzyme deficiency like so many\\n[00:01:38] things is not binary black and white,\\n[00:01:41] but rather can present and exist on a\\n[00:01:43] spectrum. One study found that 8% of\\n[00:01:46] individuals studied had this deficiency.\\n[00:01:50] And another study found as much as or as\\n[00:01:53] many as one in four. So 25% of people\\n[00:01:56] with these chronic unexplained\\n[00:01:59] gastrointestinal symptoms actually had\\n[00:02:02] this sucrace isomaltase deficiency that\\n[00:02:05] underlied their symptoms. Now why is\\n[00:02:08] that? Why is it that this enzyme\\n[00:02:10] deficiency\\n[00:02:12] triggers symptoms? Well, this is what\\n[00:02:14] you're seeing in this diagram here,\\n[00:02:17] wherein if you consume really any food\\n[00:02:20] stuff that your body can't break down\\n[00:02:23] and assimilate, it becomes food and and\\n[00:02:26] fuel for the organisms in your gut. So,\\n[00:02:30] not only do you have sort of bacterial\\n[00:02:31] fermentation and also you can fuel\\n[00:02:35] overgrowth of things like fungal\\n[00:02:37] populations, but that process can pull\\n[00:02:40] water into the intestines and lead to\\n[00:02:43] bloating, distension, and discomfort.\\n[00:02:45] And the byproducts of that fermentation\\n[00:02:48] of of the the the feeding of the\\n[00:02:49] bacteria and fungus can be noxious and\\n[00:02:52] irritating. And so this this whole\\n[00:02:54] syndrome is really what can be avoided\\n[00:02:57] and improved if we're eating the\\n[00:03:00] appropriate way for an enzyme\\n[00:03:02] deficiency. A really basic example of\\n[00:03:04] this would be someone with a lactase\\n[00:03:07] deficiency who can't digest the\\n[00:03:09] carbohydrates in dairy. If they were\\n[00:03:11] eating a high amount of of let's say\\n[00:03:14] milk or drinking milk every day, they\\n[00:03:17] would have a lot of gastrointestinal\\n[00:03:19] issues. This can present in another way\\n[00:03:22] for certain types of starches and\\n[00:03:24] certain types of sugars or or sucrose.\\n[00:03:28] And the good news here is that the diet\\n[00:03:31] to address this actually appears to be\\n[00:03:35] easier to do than some of the previous\\n[00:03:37] gut health diets that we've been talking\\n[00:03:40] about in the show and that are uh sort\\n[00:03:42] of commonly used and and well studied.\\n[00:03:45] Okay, so let's go to this study setup.\\n[00:03:49] Okay, so this study comes from a group\\n[00:03:51] of researchers at Lond University in\\n[00:03:54] Sweden. It was published in 2024\\n[00:03:58] in the journal Nutrients. Now, we've\\n[00:04:00] discussed the journal nutrients before\\n[00:04:02] because of a paper that we published in\\n[00:04:04] collaboration with Christy Adamo a few\\n[00:04:06] years back looking at the gut nutrient\\n[00:04:08] thyroid access. Just to sort of um make\\n[00:04:11] a quick mention of that. Now in this\\n[00:04:14] study from Lond University on the diet\\n[00:04:16] for this enzyme deficiency very\\n[00:04:19] importantly they took a group of 155 IBS\\n[00:04:24] patients so patients with chronic food\\n[00:04:26] reactivity and gastrointestinal\\n[00:04:28] symptoms. half were put on the low\\n[00:04:32] FODMAP diet and the other half were put\\n[00:04:36] on this SSRD\\n[00:04:39] or sucrose starch reduction diet. The\\n[00:04:43] way we can think about the sucrossse\\n[00:04:45] starch reduction diet and and we'll give\\n[00:04:47] you the specific sort of dietary\\n[00:04:49] guidelines in a second, but high level I\\n[00:04:51] view it as combining some of the best\\n[00:04:53] and most effective rules from low FODMAP\\n[00:04:57] and from low carb. But again, it does so\\n[00:05:00] in a way that ends up being less\\n[00:05:02] restrictive. And the low FOBMAP diet, in\\n[00:05:05] case you haven't heard of that, is maybe\\n[00:05:08] one of the most wellstudied and\\n[00:05:10] efficacious diets for those who have IBS\\n[00:05:14] and/or food reactivity. The low FOBMAP\\n[00:05:16] diet has been shown to be effective for\\n[00:05:18] reducing SIBO gas levels, reducing\\n[00:05:20] histamine, reducing triggering of mass\\n[00:05:22] cells, reducing leaky gut. I think it's\\n[00:05:25] a wonderful diet. We've even talked\\n[00:05:26] about research from many years ago that\\n[00:05:29] has found you can improve the density of\\n[00:05:32] serotonin cells in the gut by using this\\n[00:05:34] diet. So I would say I am a pretty big\\n[00:05:37] advocate for the low FOBMAP diet because\\n[00:05:40] it's well studied and it's effective.\\n[00:05:42] But there's one fairly important caveat\\n[00:05:46] there which is it can be difficult to\\n[00:05:48] do. This is probably the most common\\n[00:05:50] feedback we get regarding compliance.\\n[00:05:53] People tend to see responses when we\\n[00:05:56] recommend this diet in the clinic, but I\\n[00:05:58] would estimate 25 to 50% of the time\\n[00:06:01] people gripe, I think rightfully so,\\n[00:06:03] that it just feels counterintuitive,\\n[00:06:05] clunky, and hard to do. So anyway, in\\n[00:06:07] the study setup, giving you this context\\n[00:06:10] of they didn't look at the SSRD diet\\n[00:06:13] against a standard American diet because\\n[00:06:15] almost any diet is going to look\\n[00:06:17] beneficial next to garbage, right? They\\n[00:06:21] looked at it next to the low FOBMAP\\n[00:06:23] diet. So, it's a very important and\\n[00:06:26] relevant comparison. Let's talk about\\n[00:06:28] what they found. There's two levels of\\n[00:06:31] finding so to speak in this study.\\n[00:06:32] Firstly, what was found for both the\\n[00:06:35] diets and then secondly, we'll cover\\n[00:06:38] what was uniquely beneficial about the\\n[00:06:41] SSRD diet. So whether people went on low\\n[00:06:45] FODMAP or the sucrose starch reduction\\n[00:06:49] diet\\n[00:06:51] there there was about an 80% total\\n[00:06:53] response rate and even 25% of\\n[00:06:56] individuals saw complete resolution of\\n[00:06:59] their symptoms irrespective of the diet\\n[00:07:02] that they went on. again which you would\\n[00:07:04] expect given the nature of the low\\n[00:07:07] FODMAP diet being well studied and the\\n[00:07:11] fact that we know it's an effective\\n[00:07:13] diet. So again any of these diets sort\\n[00:07:15] of the the two best diets that we have\\n[00:07:17] showed a lot of improvement including\\n[00:07:20] gastrointestinal abdominal pain diarrhea\\n[00:07:24] constipation flatulence vomiting nausea\\n[00:07:28] belching reflux and food intolerances.\\n[00:07:32] Now given that both these diets are\\n[00:07:35] helpful for the gut and the gut has such\\n[00:07:38] a systemic impact there was also an\\n[00:07:41] impact on systemic symptoms headaches\\n[00:07:44] mental well-being or mood pain including\\n[00:07:47] back pain leg pain muscle pain joint\\n[00:07:50] pain and finally improvements in\\n[00:07:53] fatigue. So, so far we have two really\\n[00:07:57] good dietary options. Low FODMAP sucrose\\n[00:08:00] starch reduction. But now let's talk\\n[00:08:03] about where the SSRD diet shows the\\n[00:08:08] additional benefit. And by the way, if\\n[00:08:10] this has been helpful, please comment,\\n[00:08:12] like, subscribe, or share. This support\\n[00:08:16] is really helpful for the channel. Okay,\\n[00:08:19] so there's three points that give the\\n[00:08:22] SSRD diet the edge. Firstly, it was\\n[00:08:25] better for body composition. There was a\\n[00:08:29] 3.5\\n[00:08:31] pound additional weight loss in the SSRD\\n[00:08:35] diet group. And importantly, there\\n[00:08:37] wasn't any signal of people losing more\\n[00:08:40] weight than they wanted to or going from\\n[00:08:42] sort of normal low weight to\\n[00:08:44] underweight, but rather for those who\\n[00:08:46] needed to lose some weight, there was a\\n[00:08:48] better weight loss effect on this diet.\\n[00:08:51] Second point, there was a significant\\n[00:08:54] reduction in cravings in the SSRD diet\\n[00:08:59] group. Now, I want to show you a\\n[00:09:00] graphic. This graphic is from the same\\n[00:09:03] research group, a different study\\n[00:09:05] looking at the SSRD diet, but they just\\n[00:09:07] had a very compelling visual showing you\\n[00:09:10] the impact after 4 weeks on this diet\\n[00:09:14] regarding sugar cravings or sweet\\n[00:09:15] cravings. So, a pretty notable reduction\\n[00:09:18] in cravings. The obvious reason why that\\n[00:09:20] is so important and helpful is if you\\n[00:09:24] know what you should or should not eat,\\n[00:09:27] but you keep sneaking in food because\\n[00:09:28] you have cravings, then that makes\\n[00:09:30] compliance with a diet really hard. And\\n[00:09:32] compliance to a diet is super important.\\n[00:09:35] Now, you don't have to be 100%\\n[00:09:38] compliant, but not feeling like you're\\n[00:09:42] always having to exert a lot of\\n[00:09:44] willpower to adhere to a diet is hugely\\n[00:09:47] beneficial. And the third and final\\n[00:09:49] point, people commented that this diet,\\n[00:09:53] the SSRD or low sucrossse low starch\\n[00:09:55] diet was easier to follow. In fact, as\\n[00:09:59] part of the design for this study, some\\n[00:10:03] participants would start on one diet,\\n[00:10:06] take a break for a week or two, what's\\n[00:10:08] known as a wash out period, and then go\\n[00:10:10] on the other diet. So, some people could\\n[00:10:13] comment at their experience after doing\\n[00:10:16] both diets. Here's what the researchers\\n[00:10:18] had to say about this. Of [snorts] those\\n[00:10:21] who had tested both diets, most\\n[00:10:24] preferred the low sucrose and starch\\n[00:10:26] diet since it was easier to adhere to\\n[00:10:29] and was experienced as more efficient.\\n[00:10:33] The concept of avoiding sucrossse and\\n[00:10:35] starch is easier to adhere to in\\n[00:10:37] comparison to fermentable carbohydrates.\\n[00:10:40] This is because avoiding essentially\\n[00:10:43] high starch vegetables and high sugar\\n[00:10:46] fruits is very intuitive,\\n[00:10:48] straightforward, and easy. Whereas the\\n[00:10:52] lower FODMAP guidelines don't have a\\n[00:10:55] rhyme or a reason or there's not a easy\\n[00:10:57] visual cue that tells you if a food is\\n[00:11:01] high or low FODMAP. And therefore, you\\n[00:11:02] literally have to be checking almost\\n[00:11:05] every food either in an app or against a\\n[00:11:07] diet guide, which as you can imagine\\n[00:11:08] over time becomes exhausting and makes\\n[00:11:11] the diet feel less efficient, more\\n[00:11:14] cumbersome, and harder to do. The other\\n[00:11:16] point here I wanted to make sure to tie\\n[00:11:17] in is this is not all about the enzyme\\n[00:11:22] deficiency. If we think critically for a\\n[00:11:25] second, we see that there's probably a\\n[00:11:27] sizable impact on the microbiotic\\n[00:11:30] irrespective of enzyme deficiency. If\\n[00:11:34] the upper estimate of the prevalence of\\n[00:11:37] this gene deficiency, this enzyme\\n[00:11:39] deficiency is 25%.\\n[00:11:43] Yet 80% of people on the diet\\n[00:11:46] experienced benefit. That tells us\\n[00:11:48] something is happening beyond\\n[00:11:52] the enzyme impact. And this is likely\\n[00:11:55] due to the impact on the microbiota. So\\n[00:11:57] irrespective of the enzyatic piece which\\n[00:12:00] is only going to make things worse. If\\n[00:12:03] you consume more sucrose and more of\\n[00:12:07] certain starches then your system then\\n[00:12:10] your enzymes and your uh guts bacterial\\n[00:12:14] and fungal populations can metabolize\\n[00:12:16] you start to feed overgrowths. I just\\n[00:12:19] want to make sure to tie that in because\\n[00:12:22] it would be a misnomer to represent this\\n[00:12:25] as the this SSRD diet for only being\\n[00:12:29] beneficial for those who have this\\n[00:12:30] enzyme deficiency and likely actually\\n[00:12:34] the majority of people will not have\\n[00:12:36] this enzyme deficiency but still see\\n[00:12:39] benefit. So key point now this also begs\\n[00:12:42] the question of can you go out and do a\\n[00:12:44] test to quantify if you have this enzyme\\n[00:12:49] deficiency. Intestinal biopsy is a gold\\n[00:12:51] standard. Obviously not very practical.\\n[00:12:53] You can do a breath test where you drink\\n[00:12:56] sucrose and then you perform repeat\\n[00:12:59] breath assessments over a time period.\\n[00:13:02] However, if you have underlying small\\n[00:13:04] intestinal bacterial overgrowth, for\\n[00:13:06] example, SIBO, that can cause a false\\n[00:13:08] positive on this test. So, a more\\n[00:13:11] practical way might be a sucrose\\n[00:13:13] challenge. Just either try the diet or\\n[00:13:16] try eating a whole bunch of sucrose, see\\n[00:13:18] how you respond, and that gives you at\\n[00:13:20] least a hint. But again, beyond the\\n[00:13:23] enzyme piece, this seems to be helpful\\n[00:13:25] irrespective because it has a favorable\\n[00:13:28] impact in my opinion on modulating the\\n[00:13:32] underlying bacterial and fungal\\n[00:13:34] populations. Okay, so now let's go into\\n[00:13:37] the dietary guidelines for the SSRD\\n[00:13:39] diet. And let me lead by saying the the\\n[00:13:43] big picture overarching concept here is\\n[00:13:46] this diet has taken a couple concepts\\n[00:13:49] from low FOB map, a couple concepts from\\n[00:13:52] low carb, put them together, but not\\n[00:13:57] borrowed all of the rules from each and\\n[00:14:00] therefore what you end up with is a diet\\n[00:14:03] that seemingly takes the best from both\\n[00:14:06] worlds and gives you a diet that's\\n[00:14:09] easier to do. So, we'll break it down by\\n[00:14:13] grains, starches, fruits, and vegetables\\n[00:14:15] so that you can see which of these types\\n[00:14:18] of foods to focus on and which to avoid.\\n[00:14:21] So, starting with grains and starches,\\n[00:14:23] avoid breakfast cereals, avoid white\\n[00:14:27] bread, avoid potatoes, sweet potatoes,\\n[00:14:31] corn, and yams. Now, it's not to say you\\n[00:14:33] can never have any of that, but you want\\n[00:14:35] to generally avoid and instead focus on\\n[00:14:39] one or less servings per day of whole\\n[00:14:41] grain bread or oatmeal. And then\\n[00:14:44] separate to that, one or less servings\\n[00:14:46] per day of whole grain rice or whole\\n[00:14:49] grain pasta. Now the researchers knowing\\n[00:14:52] that there's a variance in tolerability\\n[00:14:54] did leave a crack in the door saying\\n[00:14:56] that if you don't tolerate whole grain\\n[00:15:00] you can instead use white rice or\\n[00:15:02] regular pasta if it's better tolerated.\\n[00:15:05] So an important concept which probably\\n[00:15:07] made uh or led to the observation from\\n[00:15:11] individuals in the study that it was\\n[00:15:12] efficient and easier to do is it wasn't\\n[00:15:14] super restrictive. Okay. And then with\\n[00:15:16] fruits. So, this is one where, you know,\\n[00:15:20] again, if you're shopping day overday,\\n[00:15:22] these small tweaks in buying certain\\n[00:15:26] foods and avoiding certain other foods\\n[00:15:28] can make a sizable impact in how you're\\n[00:15:31] feeling. So, fruits to avoid, apples,\\n[00:15:35] bananas, oranges, peaches, pineapples,\\n[00:15:39] cantaloupe, and honeydew. And instead,\\n[00:15:42] you have a number of fruits to focus on.\\n[00:15:45] blueberries, strawberries, blackberries.\\n[00:15:47] In fact, blackberries I've become\\n[00:15:50] personally a really big fan of lately\\n[00:15:52] because they're very high in fiber and\\n[00:15:54] also low in sugar and rich in\\n[00:15:56] polyphenols, antioxidants. Other fruits\\n[00:15:59] to focus on, lemons, limes, avocado,\\n[00:16:02] olives, cherries, grapes, kiwis, and\\n[00:16:05] pears. So, there's two areas where you\\n[00:16:08] can tweak with the grains, starches, and\\n[00:16:11] fruits that you consume. And then the\\n[00:16:14] next category, vegetables. Also, let me\\n[00:16:17] say that we will link to a full version\\n[00:16:20] of this diet guide in the description.\\n[00:16:22] And what we did and like a huge thank\\n[00:16:24] you to our team uh for working on this\\n[00:16:27] behind the scenes. So, this diet from\\n[00:16:30] the research paper was almost like an\\n[00:16:32] alphabetical list of foods that was\\n[00:16:35] quite difficult to wrap your head\\n[00:16:36] around. So, we took some time and\\n[00:16:39] partitioned as you're seeing here, you\\n[00:16:41] know, fruits versus vegetables versus\\n[00:16:44] starches versus grains so that it would\\n[00:16:47] be easier when you're shopping and and\\n[00:16:50] sort of just planning out your meals to\\n[00:16:52] adhere to this diet. And that full\\n[00:16:54] version we will have linked in the\\n[00:16:56] description uh which is going to be more\\n[00:16:58] expansive than what I'm giving you here.\\n[00:17:00] I'm just giving you here sort of the\\n[00:17:01] high level with vegetables. The\\n[00:17:03] vegetables to again generally avoid\\n[00:17:07] garlic, onion, also avoid beans, black\\n[00:17:10] eyed peas, chickpeas, green peas,\\n[00:17:12] lentils, and avoid beets, carrots,\\n[00:17:15] parsnips, and butternut squash with a\\n[00:17:18] whole bunch of foods to focus on.\\n[00:17:19] Broccoli, Brussels sprouts, cauliflower,\\n[00:17:23] artichokes, and asparagus. With one\\n[00:17:25] caveat here, consuming too high of a\\n[00:17:28] quantity of these may trigger some\\n[00:17:30] people. So, not black or white, but\\n[00:17:32] let's think on sort of a spectrum here.\\n[00:17:34] Also focusing on arugula, kale, spinach,\\n[00:17:38] chard, eggplant, pepper, tomato, celery,\\n[00:17:41] cucumber, bok joy, spaghetti squash,\\n[00:17:44] yellow squash, and zucchini. So that\\n[00:17:48] gives you a overview of again a few\\n[00:17:50] modifications that shouldn't be too hard\\n[00:17:53] to implement regarding what fruits,\\n[00:17:57] vegetables, grains, and starches you\\n[00:18:00] mostly focus on or mostly avoid. And\\n[00:18:02] again, I'm using this terminology very\\n[00:18:04] specifically because you don't have to\\n[00:18:07] be 100% compliant. In fact, the way this\\n[00:18:11] study was this um study was set up was\\n[00:18:14] one month of more formal compliance\\n[00:18:17] swiftly followed by reintroduction to\\n[00:18:20] tolerance. Also, one other obvious\\n[00:18:22] caveat I should mention. If any of the\\n[00:18:25] foods that are in the allowed columns,\\n[00:18:28] you know, trigger you, of course, don't\\n[00:18:30] eat those. And again, I just want to\\n[00:18:32] echo that this diet was adhered to for\\n[00:18:35] one month and then after that, people\\n[00:18:37] started reintroducing and just kind of\\n[00:18:40] feeling out what they could tolerate.\\n[00:18:43] Are there supplements that may be\\n[00:18:47] compatible with or or enhancing of the\\n[00:18:51] low sucrossse, low starch diet? Yes. One\\n[00:18:54] is a elemental diet. Elemental Heal as\\n[00:18:57] one example is fully SSRD compliant.\\n[00:19:01] This is one of the reasons why the\\n[00:19:03] Elemental Diet is probably so beneficial\\n[00:19:05] and we see it being beneficial for IBS,\\n[00:19:08] for SIBO, for inflammatory bowel\\n[00:19:10] disease, even for things like RA. One\\n[00:19:12] thing I would add here is if you've done\\n[00:19:16] a course on an elemental diet like\\n[00:19:18] elemental heal and then you reintroduced\\n[00:19:21] whole foods and notice that you had some\\n[00:19:24] regression or reactivity, you might want\\n[00:19:27] to transition into the SSRG diet. Also,\\n[00:19:31] you can take enzymes which may help with\\n[00:19:33] if there is some underlying enzyme\\n[00:19:36] deficiency. My recommendation here would\\n[00:19:38] be starting with or at least considering\\n[00:19:40] a broadspectctrum\\n[00:19:42] enzyme that has HCL, bile and sort of\\n[00:19:46] broad carbohydrate enzyme support plus a\\n[00:19:50] separate enzyme that is more targeted\\n[00:19:54] towards starches and sucrose. And we\\n[00:19:57] will link to two products in the\\n[00:20:00] description in case you wanted to try\\n[00:20:02] either of those. So in summary, key\\n[00:20:04] points, this sucrace isomalt taste\\n[00:20:07] deficiency appears to be more common\\n[00:20:10] than previously thought, affecting\\n[00:20:12] anywhere from 8% of the population up to\\n[00:20:14] one in four or 25%. The SSRD diet is\\n[00:20:19] helpful and appears to be better than\\n[00:20:22] the low FOBMAP diet. Not that it's a\\n[00:20:24] contest, but just giving you the context\\n[00:20:26] of this comparison trial and the\\n[00:20:28] equivalency in improvement of symptoms.\\n[00:20:32] But again, the SSRD diet was better for\\n[00:20:34] cravings, for weight loss, and was found\\n[00:20:36] to be easier to adhere to. And also and\\n[00:20:40] a little bit so more so my opinion but\\n[00:20:42] the SSRD diet is also likely helpful for\\n[00:20:47] fungal overgrowth SIBO leaky gut and the\\n[00:20:50] microbiotaa because when we get the\\n[00:20:52] feeding of the intestinal ecosystem\\n[00:20:55] correct we don't run the risk of leading\\n[00:20:59] to overgrowths of bacteria fungus the\\n[00:21:02] inflammation that they cause and\\n[00:21:04] obviously the imbalance in the\\n[00:21:07] microbiotic ecosystem. that is a result\\n[00:21:09] of this overfeeding of some of these\\n[00:21:11] populations. And I suppose the final\\n[00:21:13] point is if you try this or you've tried\\n[00:21:16] other diets and you feel stuck, you can\\n[00:21:19] always feel free to reach out to our\\n[00:21:20] clinic. This is what we love to do,\\n[00:21:22] helping people get past these sticking\\n[00:21:24] points. And if you have tried this or\\n[00:21:26] you do try this or you have any other\\n[00:21:28] thoughts or comments, please let me know\\n[00:21:30] what you think in the comments below.\\n[00:21:32] And I will talk to you next time.\\n[00:21:36] >> [music]\",\n    \"language\": \"en\",\n    \"checksum\": \"sha256:1d57c08f4addcc964daedc443ffb01079c8735d454ba4901b0c03193f8f20952\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"youtube\",\n        \"transcript\",\n        \"telegram-ingest\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"canonical_url\": \"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ElUfYaHrzyU\",\n    \"author\": \"Dr. Michael Ruscio, DC, DNM\"\n  },\n  \"required_json_shape\": {\n    \"summary\": \"Faithful concise summary of the source.\",\n    \"raw_context\": \"Cleaned normalized source content preserving all materially useful information.\",\n    \"extracted_claims\": [\n      \"Grounded claim from the source.\"\n    ],\n    \"open_questions\": [\n      \"Optional unresolved ambiguity from the source.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"constraints\": [\n    \"Preserve concrete names, dates, numbers, URLs, tool names, decisions, and caveats from the source.\",\n    \"Use extracted_claims only for claims directly supported by the source.\",\n    \"Use open_questions only for unresolved gaps present in or implied by the source.\",\n    \"If the source is thin, keep raw_context short but still faithful.\"\n  ]\n}"}],"msg":"llm: outgoing request"}
